From 94b17439074401e6a36d7dd46b7130cfa47192fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:15:16 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_sandbox: a jail hides the machine's names, pins its
 CPUs and drops what it cannot run
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo proposal §6.63 steps 1 and 3, §6.64 step 1 and the connectors
decision (operator, 2026-11-22). Four things a jailed start now does.

Host masks (§6.63 step 1). ai-jail binds /etc and /sys read-only whole
and masks hardware only under --lockdown, which Zeo cannot use (it makes
the project read-only). A jailed agent read the real hostname (UTS already
said ai-sandbox), /etc/fstab, /etc/machine-id, the boot command line (disk
encryption names), the host's uptime, every network interface name and the
board through DMI. Every jail now gets ai-jail's existing --mask on
/etc/hostname, /etc/fstab, /etc/machine-id, /proc/cmdline, /proc/uptime,
/sys/class/net, /sys/class/dmi and /sys/devices/virtual/dmi, for the paths
this machine has (ai-jail warns on a missing one and applies nothing).
ai-jail 3.5.0's --mask makes a file empty and read-only and a folder an
empty tmpfs, after every base mount, so the masks land on top of /proc and
/sys. ai-jail's --mask-host (the fork, 2026-11-22) covers none of these, so
Zeo's list carries them. Measured with the same binds (bubblewrap 0.13.0):
ps, top and ls work; `uptime` fails, which is the point. A bind over a
/proc file makes the kernel refuse nested procfs mounts, but nested
sandboxes (bwrap, podman, Claude Code's Bash sandbox) already fail inside a
default jail because of ai-jail's Landlock and seccomp (the ai-jail lane's
finding), so that costs nothing.

The XDG session variables the analysis saw leak (XDG_SESSION_*, XDG_SEAT*,
XDG_VTNR) came from running ai-jail from a login shell; a jail Zeo starts
gets an environment of PATH, LANG, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, HOME and the configured
names only (0057's launch_jail_env_is_only_the_configured_names_and_zeos_base
holds that), so nothing is added for them here.

agent_sandbox.mask_host (§6.63 step 2's Zeo half): a per-project setting,
off by default, that passes ai-jail's --mask-host (CPU and memory files
sized to the limits, only the jail's own cgroup, a per-jail machine id);
under it the empty machine-id mask is left out. The probe reads
--mask-host from ai-jail --help; a project that asks for it from an ai-jail
without it does not start (fail closed, as the limits do:
LaunchCause::MaskHostUnsupported). Zeo's disk watchdog finds the jail's
scope through /proc/<pid>/cgroup read from outside the jail, which a cgroup
namespace inside does not change (reasoned, not measured).

CPU pin (§6.63 step 3). ai-jail's quota is time, so nproc (which reads
the cgroup's cpu.max) said 4 while getconf, lscpu and every runtime asking
sched_getaffinity said 32. A quota now brings --cpus with the last
ceil(quota / 100) CPUs Zeo itself may use (from /proc/self/status), away
from CPU 0, which takes most interrupts; none when the quota covers them
all, or when the installed ai-jail has no --cpus.

claude.ai connectors (§6.64, decided 2026-11-22). They reach their vendors
through mcp-proxy.anthropic.com, so a jail that allows anthropic.com let
every one of them past allow_hosts. A jail now gets
ENABLE_CLAUDEAI_MCP_SERVERS=false, carried by name with --env (no value on
the command line); agent_sandbox.claude_ai_connectors (default false, per
project) turns them back on.

MCP servers the jail cannot start (§6.64 step 1). Zeo hands every enabled
context server to the agent in session/new, and the agent starts each
stdio one inside the jail, where only the system folders, the project and
the writable folders are at their host paths. An extension's server
(node .../extensions/work/<ext>/.../bin.js) failed there with "failed to
start ... Run /mcp", the cause only in the jail's MCP log. Such a server
is now left out of the session, the thread shows one warning notice
naming it and the path that hides it, and one log line records it
(event=mcp_server_left_out). That also keeps its arguments -- the figma
extension passes its API key as one -- out of the jail. The connection
knows its launch through StdioLaunch::launch_id (custom.rs passes it,
v1 and v2 keep it); host_view::jail_roots gives the folders the jail
shows, hidden_server_path the first one a server needs outside them
(`..` never counts as inside).

The page's command preview lists every mask (rendering reads no file
system). The rules live in a new module, host_view.rs.

Tests:
- agent_sandbox host_view::host_view_tests (new): the masks in every jail,
  the same masks plus --mask-host and no machine-id mask under mask_host,
  a missing path skipped, the CPU list
  (last N, rounded up, only allowed CPUs, none past them all), the kernel
  list parser, and the hidden-path rule with the figma case, a flag
  value, `..` and a shared prefix.
- agent_sandbox host_view_launch_tests (new, through a fake ai-jail): the
  masks, --env ENABLE_CLAUDEAI_MCP_SERVERS and --cpus before `--`; a
  project keeping its connectors; mask_host refused without the option;
  mask_host passed with every mask and without the machine-id mask; no
  pin without --cpus.
- agent_servers acp::jail_servers::jail_servers_tests (new): an extension
  server left out, a project server and an http one kept; nothing left
  out without a jail.
Red seen for every behaviour test with the new rules neutralized; the
guards (nothing pinned, nothing left out without a jail, connectors kept
on request, a server inside kept) hold either way.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
